WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.250 --> 00:00:03.230
Hello! I am your virtual teacher,

2
00:00:03.230 --> 00:00:08.830
my name is Aigul. Today we will look at the
topic of matrices.

3
00:00:10.460 --> 00:00:14.298
In real life, matrices are used not only in
mathematics

4
00:00:14.298 --> 00:00:16.600
but also in physics, engineering,

5
00:00:16.600 --> 00:00:22.740
economics and other fields, to solve systems
of equations.

6
00:00:24.120 --> 00:00:31.640
The entries of a matrix can be numbers,
algebraic symbols or mathematical functions.

7
00:00:33.270 --> 00:00:38.550
The size of a matrix is given by the number of
its rows and columns.

8
00:00:39.440 --> 00:00:41.986
To denote the size of a matrix,

9
00:00:41.986 --> 00:00:46.340
the symbols m and n are used: it means the
matrix has

10
00:00:46.340 --> 00:00:50.080
m rows and n columns.

11
00:00:52.100 --> 00:00:57.140
The matrix itself is denoted by a capital
Latin letter,

12
00:00:57.560 --> 00:01:02.280
and the table of its entries is enclosed in
parentheses.

13
00:01:04.550 --> 00:01:10.480
A matrix is written as follows, or briefly: A
= (aᵢⱼ).

14
00:01:10.480 --> 00:01:17.360
The numbers aᵢⱼ that make up the matrix are
called its entries. i is the row number,

15
00:01:17.360 --> 00:01:22.380
also called an index; j is the column number,
also an index.

16
00:01:22.980 --> 00:01:25.962
The entries on the diagonal running from the

17
00:01:25.962 --> 00:01:28.740
upper left corner form the main diagonal.

18
00:01:28.740 --> 00:01:34.600
The diagonal running from the lower left
corner is called the secondary diagonal.

19
00:01:36.240 --> 00:01:43.100
If all corresponding entries of two matrices
are equal, then the matrices

20
00:01:43.100 --> 00:01:46.140
are equal to each other, that is, A = B.

21
00:01:47.640 --> 00:01:52.295
A matrix whose number of rows equals its
number

22
00:01:52.295 --> 00:01:55.960
of columns is called a square matrix.

23
00:01:57.720 --> 00:02:04.720
A square matrix of size n is called a matrix
of order n.

24
00:02:06.580 --> 00:02:12.920
A square matrix whose entries are all zero
except those on the main diagonal

25
00:02:12.920 --> 00:02:16.380
is called a diagonal matrix.

26
00:02:16.380 --> 00:02:22.900
If the main-diagonal entries of a diagonal
matrix are all ones, such

27
00:02:22.900 --> 00:02:28.140
a matrix is called the identity matrix and is
denoted by the letter E.

28
00:02:29.710 --> 00:02:36.050
A matrix consisting of a single column or a
single row is called a vector, or

29
00:02:36.050 --> 00:02:40.170
a column matrix or a row matrix, respectively.

30
00:02:41.930 --> 00:02:45.297
The matrix obtained from a given one by
replacing

31
00:02:45.297 --> 00:02:48.390
its rows with the columns of the same numbers

32
00:02:48.390 --> 00:02:52.897
is called the transpose of the original matrix

33
00:02:52.897 --> 00:02:56.620
and is denoted Aᵀ, "A to the power T".

34
00:02:58.630 --> 00:03:04.110
Let us look at examples of transposing a
matrix. Here is a matrix A.

35
00:03:04.530 --> 00:03:08.770
Then the transposed matrix looks like this.

36
00:03:09.220 --> 00:03:14.820
Let us look at another example: if A is a
column matrix,

37
00:03:15.040 --> 00:03:20.420
then the transposed matrix A becomes a row
matrix.

38
00:03:21.700 --> 00:03:27.980
To sum up: in this lesson we learned what
matrices are and learned to identify

39
00:03:27.980 --> 00:03:30.280
their sizes and types.
